{"id": "2026-09-19-news-us-denmark-reach-greenland-security-deal", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "US-Denmark reach Greenland security deal", "summary": "Trump announced a US-Denmark-Greenland security accord boosting US military presence in Greenland, to be signed next week at the UN General Assembly pending parliamentary approval.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/zerreissprobe-fuer-die-nato-daenemark-und-usa-verkuenden-durchbruch-im-groenland-streit", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Trump announced on 19 September a US–Denmark–Greenland security accord that he says gives Washington \"permanent control\" over Greenland's defence — a framing Copenhagen pointedly did not confirm. On Truth Social, Trump wrote that the deal raises the US military presence on the Arctic island at no cost to the US and bars American adversaries from building bases or making certain investments there without US permission. Danish Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen's office said (on X) the accord — among Greenland, Denmark and the US — is due to be signed next week during the UN General Assembly, but only after both the Danish and Greenlandic parliaments approve it; Frederiksen and Greenland Premier Jens-Frederik Nielsen cast it as recognising Danish sovereignty and Greenlanders' right to self-determination while strengthening Arctic and North Atlantic security \"good for NATO and Europe.\" Crucially, the Danish and Greenlandic sides did not confirm Trump's \"permanent control\" characterisation, gave no detail on the parties' actual rights or obligations, and stressed the parliamentary steps still ahead.", "why": "Trump's months-long drive to acquire Greenland — an autonomous territory of a NATO ally — had strained the alliance toward breaking point, so a negotiated basing arrangement, if ratified, defuses that short of a US annexation; but the gap between Trump's \"control\" language and Copenhagen's careful wording means the real terms are not yet settled.", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-us-denmark-reach-greenland-security-deal", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": "2026-09-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Greenland", "Denmark", "United States", "NATO"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-air-alert-hits-riyadh-as-yemen-war-escalates", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Air alert hits Riyadh as Yemen war escalates", "summary": "Explosions and a first-ever air alert struck Riyadh amid a Houthi escalation that UNICEF says has displaced more than 57,000 Yemeni children in two weeks.", "url": "https://www.lemonde.fr/international/article/2026/09/19/arabie-saoudite-des-explosions-entendues-a-riyad-apres-une-alerte-aerienne_6777045_3210.html", "source_domain": "lemonde.fr", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["ongoing", "disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "An air-raid alert and explosions hit Riyadh for the first time early on Saturday 19 September, as Iran-aligned Houthi forces widen a fast-escalating Yemen war. AFP journalists heard two blasts after Saudi authorities warned residents by phone of a \"hostile aerial threat\" shortly before 3am and told them to stay indoors; civil defence lifted the alert about 30 minutes later, and the kingdom had not detailed the threat's origin by midday. The Houthis — who control much of Yemen and recently seized the strategic Bab al-Mandab strait (the Red Sea's southern chokepoint), imposing a blockade on Saudi tankers — have been striking Saudi Arabia for days; Riyadh accused them on 16 September of targeting the holy city of Mecca, which the Houthis denied, saying they hit only oil and military sites \"far from sacred sites\" . UNICEF says the renewed fighting has displaced more than 57,000 children in two weeks and, since 3 September, killed at least nine, in a country where 12 million children depend on aid.", "why": "with Iran's separate closure of the Strait of Hormuz already snarling global shipping, a Houthi campaign now reaching the Saudi capital risks reopening a full regional war and a fresh oil-supply shock.", "thread_id": "2026-09-17-news-yemen-fighting-displaces-57-000-children", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-17", "event_date": "2026-09-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Houthis", "Saudi Arabia", "Yemen", "Riyadh", "Iran"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-trump-signs-sweeping-russia-sanctions-law", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump signs sweeping Russia sanctions law", "summary": "Trump signed the Lindsey Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act, giving him authority to tariff the biggest buyers of Russian oil up to 100 percent.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/19/trump-signs-sweeping-russia-sanctions-over-ukraine-war", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Trump signed the sweeping Russia sanctions bill into law on Friday 18 September, days after Congress cleared it with rare bipartisan support. The \"Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026\" — named for the late Republican senator who negotiated it before his death in July — targets President Vladimir Putin, senior officials, oligarchs, Russian banks, the energy and defence sectors, and the \"shadow fleet\" of tankers used to evade earlier sanctions. Its central lever gives Trump authority to impose tariffs of up to 100% on the five largest buyers of Russian oil and gas — a burden expected to fall hardest on China and India, Russia's top crude customers — and it separately extends sanctions on Iran's energy and weapons sectors. The bill passed the House on Wednesday 16 September with 58 Democrats crossing over, the first major Ukraine-related law to clear Congress in more than two years; House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries opposed it, warning the tariff powers would raise US consumer costs and that the sanctions were full of \"loopholes.\"", "why": "the law's bite is discretionary, not automatic — it depends on Trump actually invoking the tariff authority against China and India, which have so far kept buying Russian crude.", "thread_id": "2026-09-17-news-congress-passes-graham-russia-iran-sanctions-bil", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-17", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Donald Trump", "Russia", "Ukraine", "China", "India"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-germany-votes-in-berlin-and-mecklenburg-polls", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Germany votes in Berlin and Mecklenburg polls", "summary": "Two German state elections on Sunday 20 September pit the AfD against the SPD in Mecklenburg and the CDU against Die Linke in Berlin, two weeks after the AfD's Saxony-Anhalt win.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-campaigns-wrap-up-in-two-state-elections/live-79335548", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Voters in Berlin and Mecklenburg–Western Pomerania go to the polls on Sunday 20 September in two German state elections shadowed by far-right momentum. In the northeastern state of Mecklenburg–Western Pomerania, a tight race is expected between the Alternative for Germany (AfD) and the centre-left Social Democrats (SPD) of Premier Manuela Schwesig, with the Greens hovering around the 5% threshold needed to stay in the state parliament; in Berlin, Chancellor Friedrich Merz's Christian Democrats (CDU) are neck-and-neck with the left-wing Die Linke, and the left-populist Sahra Wagenknecht Alliance (BSW) risks slipping below 5%. Public broadcaster ZDF put undecided voters at 21% in Mecklenburg and 23% in Berlin as of Thursday. The votes fall two weeks after the AfD's landslide in Saxony-Anhalt: Charlotte Knobloch, former head of Germany's Central Council of Jews, renewed her call for the party to be banned, and Berlin police searched the home of an aide to the AfD's lead Berlin candidate over suspected weapons- and explosives-law offences.", "why": "two more strong AfD showings would sharpen the strain on Germany's mainstream parties, who still refuse to govern with a party that now tops polls across the east.", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-germany-votes-in-berlin-and-mecklenburg-polls", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": "2026-09-20", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["AfD", "Germany", "Berlin", "SPD", "CDU"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-trump-bars-cnn-ms-now-politico-from-white-house", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump bars CNN, MS NOW, Politico from White House", "summary": "Trump announced an immediate ban on CNN, MS NOW and Politico reporting from the White House, accusing them of biased 'fake news' coverage.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/19/can-trump-ban-cnn-news-outlets-he-doesnt-like-from-the-white-house", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Trump announced on Friday 18 September an immediate ban on CNN, MS NOW (formerly MSNBC) and Politico reporting from the White House, accusing them of \"fake news.\". Pressed by reporters, he said the outlets \"purposely write negative news\" to \"diminish\" Republicans, and signalled the ban could widen, calling The New York Times and The Washington Post \"fake news\" too; CNN rejected the move and said it stands behind its White House team's \"fair and accurate reporting.\"", "why": "barring specific outlets from the White House press corps over unfavourable coverage is unprecedented and, press-freedom advocates argue, runs into First Amendment protections — making it a live test of how far a president can choose which journalists may cover him.", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-trump-bars-cnn-ms-now-politico-from-white-house", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Donald Trump", "CNN", "Politico", "White House"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-suicide-assault-on-kohat-police-hq-kills-31", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Suicide assault on Kohat police HQ kills 31", "summary": "A TTP-linked faction stormed a police headquarters in Kohat, northwest Pakistan, killing at least 31 including 16 officers.", "url": "https://www.lemonde.fr/international/article/2026/09/19/pakistan-au-moins-31-morts-dans-l-attaque-d-un-quartier-general-de-la-police-a-kohat_6777327_3210.html", "source_domain": "lemonde.fr", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["security", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "At least 31 people, including 16 police officers, were killed when militants stormed a police headquarters in Kohat, northwest Pakistan, in an assault that began on Friday 18 September and continued into Saturday. A suicide bomber breached the district police complex — which houses counter-terrorism and special-branch offices — in the city of about 220,000 in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, and gunmen were still holed up inside on Saturday. A faction linked to the Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP, the Pakistani Taliban — a separate but allied outfit to the Afghan Taliban) claimed the attack, which came a day after a roadside bomb killed six soldiers in neighbouring Hangu district.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-suicide-assault-on-kohat-police-hq-kills-31", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Pakistan", "Kohat", "Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan", "Khyber Pakhtunkhwa"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-eu-floats-crisis-limits-on-asylum-access-after-c", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "EU floats crisis limits on asylum access after Ceuta", "summary": "The European Commission unveiled crisis rules letting member states depart from normal asylum standards during a mass influx, a Dublin-relevant shift for Switzerland.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/neue-asyl-krisenregeln-nach-ceuta-will-die-eu-den-asylrechtlichen-spielraum-ausreizen", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The European Commission this week unveiled \"crisis\" rules that would let member states depart from normal asylum standards during a mass influx of migrants — a Schengen-relevant shift that reaches Switzerland through the Dublin system. Commission President Ursula von der Leyen announced the plans after the late-July surge at Spain's North African enclave of Ceuta; EU migration commissioner Magnus Brunner, in an SRF interview, said states could \"selectively\" restrict access to asylum procedures in such situations but acknowledged that key rule-of-law questions — including whether the rules would in effect legalise \"pushbacks,\" i.e. turning people away without an asylum hearing — remain unresolved. The story sits directly on Switzerland's borders: Italy has for nearly four years failed to take back asylum seekers it is responsible for under Dublin, Germany runs checks at the Swiss border to Bern's irritation, and the SVP argues Switzerland should be allowed to do the same.", "why": "any loosening of EU asylum standards flows into the Dublin framework Switzerland relies on, shaping who can be returned where — and hands domestic hardliners a fresh precedent to cite.", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-eu-floats-crisis-limits-on-asylum-access-after-c", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Commission", "Switzerland", "Ursula von der Leyen", "Magnus Brunner", "Ceuta"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-swiss-church-exits-fall-but-membership-shrinks", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss church exits fall but membership shrinks", "summary": "Departures from Switzerland's main churches dropped sharply in 2025 even as overall membership kept declining, new church statistics show.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/taufquote-sinkt-kirchen-verlieren-weiter-mitglieder-aber-auf-tieferem-niveau", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Departures from Switzerland's main churches fell sharply in 2025 even as overall membership kept shrinking, the latest church statistics show. According to the St. Gallen Pastoral Sociology Institute (SPI), 27,878 people left the Roman Catholic Church nationwide — roughly 9,000 fewer than in 2024 — and 26,243 left the Evangelical Reformed Church, about 6,300 fewer. Both figures are far below the 2023 exit wave that followed the publication of a Catholic-Church abuse study, when departures ran roughly twice as high. The institute called the drop in exits \"surprisingly strong,\" though it noted that membership is still declining overall.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-swiss-church-exits-fall-but-membership-shrinks", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Roman Catholic Church", "Evangelical Reformed Church", "SPI"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-geneva-flyers-now-pick-china-over-the-us", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Geneva flyers now pick China over the US", "summary": "For the first time, Geneva Airport handled more passengers to and from China than to and from the United States this summer.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/au-depart-de-geneve-on-prefere-desormais-voler-vers-la-chine-plutot-que-vers-les-etats-unis", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "For the first time in Geneva Airport's history, more passengers flew to and from China than to and from the United States this summer. A Genève Aéroport spokesperson called the reversal \"spectacular,\" noting that in the same months a year earlier the airport handled twice as many US-bound passengers as China-bound ones. The observation, in a Le Temps column, tracks a broader shift in where Swiss travellers and connections are heading as US travel demand softens.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-geneva-flyers-now-pick-china-over-the-us", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Geneva Airport", "China", "United States", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-09-19-news-swiss-drought-deepens-with-no-rain-in-sight", "date": "2026-09-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss drought deepens with no rain in sight", "summary": "Persistent high pressure is worsening Switzerland's drought, with no rain forecast for the Plateau and soils still too dry on the northern side of the Alps.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/meteo/meteo-stories/keine-entspannung-trockene-aussichten", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "science"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Switzerland's drought is deepening, with no rain in the near-term forecast for the Plateau, meteorologists warn. Persistent high pressure is set to keep the weather dry \"until further notice\"; despite scattered fronts and thunderstorms in recent weeks, soils on the northern side of the Alps remain markedly too dry, even as above-average rainfall in parts of Ticino and the Engadine has eased conditions in the south.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-19-news-swiss-drought-deepens-with-no-rain-in-sight", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-19", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Swiss Plateau", "Ticino"]}
